description:
pkix-revocation/src/ocsp.rs:204-213

`hash_certid_input` uses an if-else chain to dispatch on OID:

```rust
fn hash_certid_input(oid: &der::asn1::ObjectIdentifier, data: &[u8]) -> crate::Result<Vec<u8>> {
    if oid == &OID_SHA1 {
        use sha1::Digest as _;
        Ok(sha1::Sha1::digest(data).to_vec())
    } else if oid == &OID_SHA256 {
        use sha2::Digest as _;
        Ok(sha2::Sha256::digest(data).to_vec())
    } else {
        Err(Error::OcspMalformed)
    }
}
```

Rust idiom strongly prefers `match` for exhaustive dispatch, especially when the default arm is the catch-all error. The `match` form is both more idiomatic and more readable, and scales gracefully when SHA-384 is added in v0.2:

```rust
fn hash_certid_input(oid: &der::asn1::ObjectIdentifier, data: &[u8]) -> crate::Result<Vec<u8>> {
    match *oid {
        OID_SHA1 => {
            use sha1::Digest as _;
            Ok(sha1::Sha1::digest(data).to_vec())
        }
        OID_SHA256 => {
            use sha2::Digest as _;
            Ok(sha2::Sha256::digest(data).to_vec())
        }
        _ => Err(Error::OcspMalformed),
    }
}
```

P4: style-only, no behavior change.
